Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
Issue 1: Users cannot log in

# Check user status in the Users interface
# Ensure passwords meet complexity requirements
# Verify user type is set correctly
Issue 2: Permissions not working as expected

Verify team membership is correctly assigned
Check that permissions are assigned to the correct resource type
Ensure the organization context is correct
Remember that permissions are additive - users get permissions from both individual assignments and team memberships
Issue 3: Templates not visible to users

Check that users have appropriate permissions on the underlying resources (project, inventory, credentials)
Verify template-specific permissions are assigned
Ensure the template is associated with the correct organization
Issue 4: Cannot execute job templates

Verify users have "Execute" permission on the template
Check that users have "Use" permission on associated project and inventory
Ensure proper credentials are assigned and accessible
Verification Commands
To verify your setup from the command line (if you have shell access):

# Check AWX/Controller service status
sudo systemctl status automation-controller

# View user creation in logs
sudo journalctl -u automation-controller | grep -i user

# Check database for user entries (if using PostgreSQL)
sudo -u postgres psql awx -c "SELECT username, email, is_active FROM auth_user;"
Best Practices Implemented
Throughout this lab, you've implemented several security best practices:

Principle of Least Privilege: Users only receive the minimum permissions necessary for their role
Role-Based Access Control: Permissions are assigned based on job functions rather than individuals
Separation of Duties: Development and production environments have different access controls
Audit Trail: All actions are logged and auditable
Team-Based Management: Using teams simplifies permission management at scale
Conclusion
In this comprehensive lab, you have successfully:

Created a structured user management system with four different user types representing common organizational roles
Implemented team-based access control that reflects real-world organizational structures
Configured granular permissions that enforce separation between development and production environments
Tested role-based access control by logging in as different users and verifying their access levels
Established security best practices including least privilege access and proper audit controls
This access control framework is crucial for enterprise automation environments because it:

Prevents unauthorized access to critical production systems
Enables compliance with security policies and regulatory requirements
Supports scalable user management through team-based permissions
Provides audit capabilities for security and compliance reporting
Reduces security risks by limiting user access to only necessary resources
The skills you've developed in this lab are directly applicable to the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Managing Automation with Ansible Automation Platform exam and are essential for managing enterprise automation platforms in production environments. Understanding how to properly configure access control is fundamental to maintaining secure, compliant, and well-governed automation infrastructure.

Remember that access control is an ongoing process - regularly review user permissions, remove access for users who no longer need it, and adjust permissions as organizational roles and responsibilities change.
